IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `safw-dates`
IMPORT `safw-core`
IMPORT `safw-seat`

-- Fair Work Act 1994 (SA) -- cases for the employee's seat (safw-seat.l4), with a few on safw-core.l4 functions the
-- seat composes (contract of employment, the s 4(4) indexation, the regulative time limits run directly). Every
-- expected value was worked by hand from the deposited text (the section is named on each group), never copied from
-- the encoding's output. Edges: the last day of every time limit and the day after (6 years, 6 months, 21 days, 2 years,
-- 3 months), 29 February and month ends, a threshold exactly met and one dollar under, a cap where both limbs are equal,
-- a designated employer paying on day 14 and day 15, the Schedule 8 anniversaries and the 45th birthday itself, each
-- limb of every "or" and each conjunct of every "and", and the order in which provisions displace one another.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Cases: the employee`

GIVEN d IS A NUMBER, m IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
dmy d m y MEANS `safw date` d m y

§§ `s 5 outworker`

-- packing at home for a business: s 5(1)(a)
#ASSERT `Am I an outworker` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- the same packing done at a factory: s 5(1) needs a residence or non-business premises
#ASSERT `Am I an outworker` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE
-- distributing work to outworkers, anywhere: s 5(2)
#ASSERT `Am I an outworker` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- not engaged for another's trade or business
#ASSERT `Am I an outworker` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE
-- cleaning a third person's home: s 5(3)
#ASSERT `Am I an outworker` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS FALSE

§§ `ss 4, 5(4), 6: does the Act apply`

`common law` MEANS `a contract recognised at common law as a contract of employment, for remuneration in an industry`
`driver` MEANS `an engagement to drive a vehicle not registered in my name to provide a public passenger service`
`cleaner` MEANS `an engagement to carry out personally the work of cleaning premises`
`outworker job` MEANS `an engagement to carry out work as an outworker`
`other job` MEANS `some other engagement`

`applies` MEANS `yes: you are an employee under this Act (s 4(1)), and s 6 does not take your employment out of the Act`
`applies ESM` MEANS `yes, but only in relation to an excluded subject matter: your employer is a national system employer (s 6(aa))`
`not employee` MEANS `no: you are not employed under a contract of employment as s 4(1) defines it, and you are not a public employee, so you are not an employee under this Act`
`5(4) no` MEANS `no: you are an outworker, and outside Chapter 1 the Act applies to outworkers only if an award or enterprise agreement provision relates to outworkers or a regulation extends the Act to them (s 5(4))`
`family no` MEANS `no: you are employed by your spouse, domestic partner or parent (s 6(a))`
`regs no` MEANS `no: the regulations exclude your employment from the Act (s 6(b))`
`national no` MEANS `no: your employer is a national system employer under the Commonwealth Fair Work Act 2009, and the matter is not an excluded subject matter (s 6(aa))`

-- s 4(1) contract of employment, (a)-(d) and the taxi exception
#ASSERT `safw is a contract of employment (s 4(1))` `driver` FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `safw is a contract of employment (s 4(1))` `driver` TRUE FALSE EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `safw is a contract of employment (s 4(1))` `cleaner` FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `safw is a contract of employment (s 4(1))` `outworker job` FALSE TRUE EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `safw is a contract of employment (s 4(1))` `outworker job` FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `safw is a contract of employment (s 4(1))` `other job` FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- a council worker on a common law contract, State-system employer
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my employment` `common law` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `applies`
-- a private sector employee of a national system employer
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my employment` `common law` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `national no`
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my employment` `common law` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `applies ESM`
-- a taxi driver who is not a common law employee
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my employment` `driver` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not employee`
-- a public employee is an employee whatever the engagement
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my employment` `other job` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `applies`
-- s 6(a), s 6(b)
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my employment` `common law` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `family no`
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my employment` `common law` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `regs no`
-- an outworker: s 5(4)
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my employment` `outworker job` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `5(4) no`
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my employment` `outworker job` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `applies`

§§ `s 17(2)`

#ASSERT `Can I bring my application to SAET` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: you may bring the application as of right, because another provision of this Act authorises it (s 17(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I bring my application to SAET` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: you may bring the application, because SAET is satisfied that the claim arises out of a genuine industrial grievance and that no other impartial grievance resolution process is or has been reasonably available to you (s 17(2)(a), (b))`
#ASSERT `Can I bring my application to SAET` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: unless another provision of this Act authorises the application, you must satisfy SAET both that the claim arises out of a genuine industrial grievance and that no other impartial grievance resolution process is or has been reasonably available to you (s 17(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I bring my application to SAET` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: unless another provision of this Act authorises the application, you must satisfy SAET both that the claim arises out of a genuine industrial grievance and that no other impartial grievance resolution process is or has been reasonably available to you (s 17(2))`

§§ `ss 9, 10, 33: can the Court hear it`

`award sum` MEANS `a sum due to me from my employer or former employer under this Act, an award, an enterprise agreement or my contract of employment`
`workplace law sum` MEANS `a sum due to me from my employer or former employer under a workplace law`
`super compensation` MEANS `compensation from my employer or former employer for failing to make superannuation contributions for my benefit`
`trustee benefit` MEANS `payment of a benefit from the trustee of a superannuation fund to which contributions have been made`
`contract debt` MEANS `a liquidated sum or debt under my common law contract of employment`
`contract damages` MEANS `damages for breach of my common law contract of employment`
`other claim` MEANS `some other claim`

`hear 9(1)(a)` MEANS `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear it as a monetary claim under s 9(1)(a)`
`not now 33` MEANS `not now: proceedings based on the same claim have begun in another court and have not been withdrawn or struck out, so the Court may not hear it (s 33)`

#ASSERT `Can the Court hear my claim` `award sum` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `hear 9(1)(a)`
#ASSERT `Can the Court hear my claim` `award sum` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not now 33`
#ASSERT `Can the Court hear my claim` `super compensation` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear it as a monetary claim under s 9(1)(c), and may direct that the compensation be paid into a superannuation fund for you (s 35(2))`
#ASSERT `Can the Court hear my claim` `trustee benefit` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear it as a monetary claim against the trustee under s 9(1)(d)`
#ASSERT `Can the Court hear my claim` `contract debt` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear it as a monetary claim for a sum or debt under s 10(1)(b)`
#ASSERT `Can the Court hear my claim` `contract debt` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not now 33`
-- damages under s 10(1)(a) are not a monetary claim, so s 33 does not stop them
#ASSERT `Can the Court hear my claim` `contract damages` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the South Australian Employment Court can hear a claim for damages for breach of the contract under s 10(1)(a); it is not a monetary claim under s 30, so ss 31, 33 and 36 do not apply to it`
#ASSERT `Can the Court hear my claim` `other claim` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: the claim is not one of the kinds in s 9(1) or s 10(1)`
-- workplace law: s 9(2) first, then the 2025 transitional clause, then s 33
#ASSERT `Can the Court hear my claim` `workplace law sum` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the workplace law gives a court jurisdiction over this claim, so it cannot be made under s 9(1); bring it under that law (s 9(2))`
#ASSERT `Can the Court hear my claim` `workplace law sum` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: a claim under a workplace law is in s 9(1) only for conduct after 1 January 2026 (Fair Work (Worker Entitlements) Amendment Act 2025, Sch 1 cl 4); the earlier text is not deposited`
#ASSERT `Can the Court hear my claim` `workplace law sum` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `not now 33`
#ASSERT `Can the Court hear my claim` `workplace law sum` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `hear 9(1)(a)`

§§ `s 31: the 6 years`

`in time 31` MEANS `in time: the claim was made within 6 years after the sum became payable (s 31)`
`out 31` MEANS `out of time: more than 6 years have passed since the sum became payable (s 31)`

#ASSERT `By what date must I make my monetary claim` (dmy 15 3 2020) EQUALS dmy 15 3 2026
#ASSERT `By what date must I make my monetary claim` (dmy 29 2 2020) EQUALS dmy 28 2 2026
#ASSERT `Is my monetary claim in time` `award sum` (dmy 15 3 2020) (dmy 15 3 2026) EQUALS `in time 31`
#ASSERT `Is my monetary claim in time` `award sum` (dmy 15 3 2020) (dmy 16 3 2026) EQUALS `out 31`
#ASSERT `Is my monetary claim in time` `award sum` (dmy 15 3 2020) (dmy 15 3 2020) EQUALS `in time 31`
#ASSERT `Is my monetary claim in time` `award sum` (dmy 29 2 2020) (dmy 28 2 2026) EQUALS `in time 31`
#ASSERT `Is my monetary claim in time` `award sum` (dmy 29 2 2020) (dmy 1 3 2026) EQUALS `out 31`
#ASSERT `Is my monetary claim in time` `award sum` (dmy 15 3 2020) (dmy 14 3 2020) EQUALS `too early: the sum had not become payable when the claim was made`
#ASSERT `Is my monetary claim in time` `super compensation` (dmy 1 7 2000) (dmy 1 7 2026) EQUALS `no time limit: none applies to a claim for the non-payment of superannuation contributions (s 31)`
-- a trustee benefit (s 9(1)(d)) keeps the 6 years (fork F4)
#ASSERT `Is my monetary claim in time` `trustee benefit` (dmy 1 7 2018) (dmy 2 7 2024) EQUALS `out 31`
#ASSERT `Is my monetary claim in time` `contract debt` (dmy 1 7 2018) (dmy 1 7 2024) EQUALS `in time 31`
#ASSERT `Is my monetary claim in time` `contract damages` (dmy 1 7 2010) (dmy 1 7 2024) EQUALS `s 31 does not apply: the claim is not a monetary claim (s 30)`
#ASSERT `Is my monetary claim in time` `other claim` (dmy 1 7 2010) (dmy 1 7 2024) EQUALS `s 31 does not apply: the claim is not a monetary claim (s 30)`
-- the s 31 regulative rule run directly: day 2191 is the last day of 6 years from 15 March 2020
#ASSERT `safw days from` (dmy 15 3 2020) `to` (dmy 15 3 2026) EQUALS 2191
#ASSERT `safw the step on this day keeps the rule` (`s 31 -- a monetary claim must be made within 6 years after the sum claimed became payable` 2191) `I, the claimant` `make the monetary claim (s 31)` 2191 EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `safw the step on this day keeps the rule` (`s 31 -- a monetary claim must be made within 6 years after the sum claimed became payable` 2191) `I, the claimant` `make the monetary claim (s 31)` 2192 EQUALS FALSE

§§ `s 32: who may claim`

#ASSERT `Who may make the monetary claim` `I am the claimant, and an adult` EQUALS `yes: the employee or former employee to whom the sum is due may make the claim (s 9(1), s 17(1)(c))`
#ASSERT `Who may make the monetary claim` `I am the claimant, and under 18` EQUALS `yes: a minor may make a monetary claim as if the minor had attained the age of majority (s 32(2))`
#ASSERT `Who may make the monetary claim` `an association making the claim on the claimant's behalf` EQUALS `yes: an association may make a monetary claim on the claimant's behalf (s 32(1))`
#ASSERT `Who may make the monetary claim` `the personal representative of the claimant, who has died` EQUALS `yes: a claim for money that should have been paid to or for a person who is now dead may be made by the personal representative (s 32(3))`
#ASSERT `Who may make the monetary claim` `a beneficiary of the estate of the claimant, who has died` EQUALS `yes: a claim for money that should have been paid to or for a person who is now dead may be made by a beneficiary of the estate (s 32(3))`
#ASSERT `Who may make the monetary claim` `someone else making the claim` EQUALS `not covered: s 32 does not name you as someone who may make the claim`

§§ `s 34: interest`

`interest yes` MEANS `yes: the judgment must include interest, or a lump sum instead of interest, taking into account the period from the day the cause of action arose to the day of judgment; there is no interest on interest (s 34(1), (1a), (2)(a))`

#ASSERT `Will my judgment include interest` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `interest yes`
#ASSERT `Will my judgment include interest` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the Court includes interest only on the application of the party in whose favour the judgment is made (s 34(1))`
#ASSERT `Will my judgment include interest` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: the Court has found good reason not to include interest (s 34(1))`
#ASSERT `Will my judgment include interest` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the judgment was given by consent, and the parties have not consented to interest (s 34(2)(c))`
#ASSERT `Will my judgment include interest` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `interest yes`
#ASSERT `Over how many days must the interest be worked out` (dmy 1 1 2025) (dmy 1 1 2026) EQUALS 365
#ASSERT `Over how many days must the interest be worked out` (dmy 1 1 2024) (dmy 1 1 2025) EQUALS 366

§§ `s 35: payment`

#ASSERT `How may the money be paid` `super compensation` EQUALS `the Court may authorise or direct payment by instalments (s 35(1)), and may direct that compensation for unpaid superannuation contributions be paid to a superannuation fund for you (s 35(2))`
#ASSERT `How may the money be paid` `award sum` EQUALS `the Court may authorise or direct payment by instalments (s 35(1))`
#ASSERT `How may the money be paid` `contract damages` EQUALS `the Court may authorise or direct payment by instalments (s 35(1))`
#ASSERT `How may the money be paid` `other claim` EQUALS `not covered: s 35 applies to claims under Chapter 2 Part 1 for a sum due or other monetary amount (s 35(3))`

§§ `s 36: costs`

`s 10 costs` MEANS `s 36 does not apply to a claim under s 10: costs are awarded as in a corresponding claim in the District Court or the Magistrates Court (s 36(3), s 10(6))`
`appeal costs possible` MEANS `possibly: on an appeal a party may be ordered to pay another party's costs, because the Court is satisfied that it instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause, or that its unreasonable act or omission caused the costs (s 36(1)(b), (2))`
`appeal costs no` MEANS `no: on an appeal a party may be ordered to pay another party's costs only if the Court is satisfied that it instituted the proceedings vexatiously or without reasonable cause, or that its unreasonable act or omission caused the costs (s 36(2))`

#ASSERT `Can I be ordered to pay costs on my monetary claim` `award sum` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: at first instance the Court may award costs on a monetary claim only to cover a claimant's reasonable expenses of establishing the present value of lost superannuation, never against a claimant (s 36(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I be ordered to pay costs on my monetary claim` `award sum` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `appeal costs possible`
#ASSERT `Can I be ordered to pay costs on my monetary claim` `award sum` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `appeal costs possible`
#ASSERT `Can I be ordered to pay costs on my monetary claim` `award sum` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `appeal costs no`
#ASSERT `Can I be ordered to pay costs on my monetary claim` `contract debt` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `s 10 costs`
#ASSERT `Can I be ordered to pay costs on my monetary claim` `contract damages` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 10 costs`
#ASSERT `Can I be ordered to pay costs on my monetary claim` `other claim` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered: the claim is not a monetary claim (s 30)`
#ASSERT `Can I recover my costs of my monetary claim` `super compensation` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `possibly: the Court may award you costs to cover the reasonable expenses you incurred to establish the present value of the loss (s 36(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can I recover my costs of my monetary claim` `award sum` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: at first instance the Court may award costs on a monetary claim only to cover a claimant's reasonable expenses of establishing the present value of lost superannuation (s 36(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can I recover my costs of my monetary claim` `award sum` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `appeal costs possible`
#ASSERT `Can I recover my costs of my monetary claim` `trustee benefit` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `appeal costs no`
#ASSERT `Can I recover my costs of my monetary claim` `contract debt` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 10 costs`

§§ `s 72B: severance`

`me` MEANS `I, the employee, alone or as one of a group of employees`
`notice in time` MEANS `yes, in time: you applied within 21 days after you were given notice of the pending redundancy; SAET may apply the minimum standard for severance payments as it thinks fit, and need not make an order (s 72B(5)-(7))`
`termination in time` MEANS `yes, in time: you applied within 21 days after your employment was terminated for redundancy; SAET may apply the minimum standard for severance payments as it thinks fit, and need not make an order (s 72B(5)-(7))`
`severance late` MEANS `no: the application was not made within 21 days after the notice was given or the employment was terminated (s 72B(6)(b))`

-- notice on 1 March 2026: the 21st day after is 22 March
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a severance order` `me` TRUE (dmy 1 3 2026) FALSE (dmy 1 1 2000) (dmy 22 3 2026) EQUALS `notice in time`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a severance order` `me` TRUE (dmy 1 3 2026) FALSE (dmy 1 1 2000) (dmy 23 3 2026) EQUALS `severance late`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a severance order` `me` TRUE (dmy 1 3 2026) FALSE (dmy 1 1 2000) (dmy 1 3 2026) EQUALS `notice in time`
-- before the notice is given, no window is open
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a severance order` `me` TRUE (dmy 1 3 2026) FALSE (dmy 1 1 2000) (dmy 28 2 2026) EQUALS `severance late`
-- terminated 10 March 2026: the 21st day after is 31 March
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a severance order` `me` FALSE (dmy 1 1 2000) TRUE (dmy 10 3 2026) (dmy 31 3 2026) EQUALS `termination in time`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a severance order` `me` FALSE (dmy 1 1 2000) TRUE (dmy 10 3 2026) (dmy 1 4 2026) EQUALS `severance late`
-- notice 1 March, terminated 10 March, applied 25 March: the notice window has closed, the termination window is open
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a severance order` `a registered association acting for me or my group` TRUE (dmy 1 3 2026) TRUE (dmy 10 3 2026) (dmy 25 3 2026) EQUALS `termination in time`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a severance order` `me` FALSE (dmy 1 3 2026) FALSE (dmy 10 3 2026) (dmy 5 3 2026) EQUALS `no: an application may be made only after notice of a pending redundancy has been given or the employment has been terminated for redundancy (s 72B(6)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply for a severance order` `someone else applying` TRUE (dmy 1 3 2026) FALSE (dmy 1 1 2000) (dmy 5 3 2026) EQUALS `not covered: s 72B(5) names only an employee, a group of employees, or a registered association acting for them`

§§ `Part 3A Division 3: outworkers`

`ow yes` MEANS `yes: your unpaid remuneration claim is validly made and in time; the apparent responsible contractor is liable for the amount claimed, unless within 14 days it refers the claim to your employer and the employer accepts liability by paying you (ss 99D, 99E, 99F)`
`ow late` MEANS `no: the claim was not made within 6 months after the work was completed (s 99D(3))`
`ow incomplete` MEANS `not yet made: the claim is made by serving a written notice that claims payment and sets out your name, your contact address, a description of the work, the dates it was done and the amount claimed, with the particulars verified by statutory declaration (s 99D(4), (5))`

#ASSERT `By what date must I serve my unpaid remuneration claim` (dmy 31 8 2026) EQUALS dmy 28 2 2027
#ASSERT `By what date must I serve my unpaid remuneration claim` (dmy 31 8 2027) EQUALS dmy 29 2 2028
#ASSERT `By what date must I serve my unpaid remuneration claim` (dmy 15 9 2026) EQUALS dmy 15 3 2027

-- completed 31 August 2026: last day 28 February 2027
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE (dmy 1 8 2026) (dmy 31 8 2026) (dmy 28 2 2027) TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `ow yes`
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE (dmy 1 8 2026) (dmy 31 8 2026) (dmy 1 3 2027) TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `ow late`
-- served before the work is completed: not late (fork F6)
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE (dmy 1 8 2026) (dmy 31 8 2026) (dmy 20 8 2026) TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `ow yes`
-- each particular, and the statutory declaration
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE (dmy 1 8 2026) (dmy 31 8 2026) (dmy 1 9 2026) FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `ow incomplete`
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE (dmy 1 8 2026) (dmy 31 8 2026) (dmy 1 9 2026) TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `ow incomplete`
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE (dmy 1 8 2026) (dmy 31 8 2026) (dmy 1 9 2026) TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `ow incomplete`
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE (dmy 1 8 2026) (dmy 31 8 2026) (dmy 1 9 2026) TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `ow incomplete`
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE (dmy 1 8 2026) (dmy 31 8 2026) (dmy 1 9 2026) TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `ow incomplete`
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE (dmy 1 8 2026) (dmy 31 8 2026) (dmy 1 9 2026) TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `ow incomplete`
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE (dmy 1 8 2026) (dmy 31 8 2026) (dmy 1 9 2026) TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `ow incomplete`
-- s 99D(6): work before the commencement on 16 May 2005
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE (dmy 15 5 2005) (dmy 20 5 2005) (dmy 1 6 2005) TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: a claim may only be made for work performed after s 99D commenced on 16 May 2005 (s 99D(6))`
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE (dmy 16 5 2005) (dmy 20 5 2005) (dmy 1 6 2005) TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `ow yes`
-- who can claim, against whom
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE (dmy 1 8 2026) (dmy 31 8 2026) (dmy 1 9 2026) TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: only an outworker may make an unpaid remuneration claim (s 99D(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE (dmy 1 8 2026) (dmy 31 8 2026) (dmy 1 9 2026) TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: as an outworker you can use Part 3A only if an award or enterprise agreement provision relates to outworkers or a regulation extends the Act to them; outside Chapter 1 the Act applies to outworkers only then (s 5(4))`
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE (dmy 1 8 2026) (dmy 31 8 2026) (dmy 1 9 2026) TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: a person whose sole business in the clothing industry is retail sale is not a responsible contractor (s 99B(3))`
#ASSERT `Can I make an unpaid remuneration claim` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE (dmy 1 8 2026) (dmy 31 8 2026) (dmy 1 9 2026) TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `not covered: s 99D(1) lets you claim only against a person you believe on reasonable grounds to be a responsible contractor for you, one who initiated an order for the work (other than as a retail purchaser) or distributed it (s 99B(1))`

